+\begin{proposition}[Torus intersections, exact]\label{prop:torus}
+For \emph{real} $k$, write $x=e^{i\theta}$, $y=e^{i\phi}$. A point
+$(x,y)$ of the torus lies on $S_k=0$ if and only if
+\[
+  (k+2\cos\theta)\sin(\theta/2)=0
+  \quad\text{and}\quad
+  |k+2\cos\theta|\,|\cos(\theta/2)|\le 2 .
+\]
+Consequently:
+\begin{enumerate}
+  \item the torus meets the curve if and only if $k\in[-4,2]$;
+  \item for $-2\le k\le2$ the \emph{fold points}
+  $\theta=\pm\arccos(-k/2)$ occur, where $B=x^2+kx+1=0$ and $y^2=-x^3$ has
+  both roots of modulus $1$;
+  \item for $-4\le k\le0$ an additional \emph{branch-exchange intersection}
+  at $\theta=0$ occurs, where $S_k(1,y)=y^2+(k+2)y+1$ has both roots on the
+  unit circle --- distinct for $-4<k<0$, a double root (tangency) at $k=-4$
+  ($y=1$) and at $k=0$ ($y=-1$);
+  \item at $k=2$ the folds coalesce with the boundary $\theta=\pi$, where
+  $S_2(-1,y)=y^2-1$.
+\end{enumerate}
+This matches Samart's observation $K\cap\R=[-4,2]$ for the
+torus-intersection parameter interval \cite{Samart2023}.
+\end{proposition}
+
+\begin{proof}
+Since $x+x^{-1}=2\cos\theta$, one has $B=x^2+kx+1=x(k+2\cos\theta)$.
+Dividing $e^{2i\phi}+Be^{i\phi}+e^{3i\theta}=0$ by $e^{i\phi}$ gives
+$e^{i\phi}+e^{i(3\theta-\phi)}=-B$, i.e.\
+\[
+  2\cos\psi\,e^{3i\theta/2}=-(k+2\cos\theta)\,e^{i\theta},
+  \qquad \psi:=\phi-3\theta/2\in\R.
+\]
+Multiplying by $e^{-i\theta}$: $2\cos\psi\,e^{i\theta/2}$ must be the real
+number $-(k+2\cos\theta)$, so $(k+2\cos\theta)\sin(\theta/2)=0$ and
+$|k+2\cos\theta||\cos(\theta/2)|=|2\cos\psi|\le2$; conversely any
+$\theta$ satisfying these gives $\cos\psi\in[-1,1]$, hence $\phi$. On
+$\theta\in[-\pi,\pi]$, $\sin(\theta/2)=0$ only at $\theta=0$, yielding
+(iii) with condition $|k+2|\le2$; the factor $k+2\cos\theta=0$ is solvable
+iff $|k|\le2$, yielding (ii). The union of the two ranges is $[-4,2]$;
+the degeneracies are read off directly.
+\end{proof}
